<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman conquest of Britain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/43">43</a>–84 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> began with the invasion ordered by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> in 43 CE and continued in stages over the next four decades. Southern Britain was occupied quickly, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> faced resistance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> and the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> in 60–61 before subduing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> Graupius in 83 or 84 marked the furthest advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and Britain remained a Roman province until about 410.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-conquest-of-britain.jpg" alt="Roman conquest of Britain" width="320" height="598" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: my work · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman.Britain.campaigns.43.to.84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s expeditions of 55 and 54 BCE left some southern British chiefdoms as allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and for the next century the island stayed in a relationship of tribute, hostages and client states without direct occupation. By 40 CE the Catuvellauni had become the strongest kingdom in the south-east and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> conquered the kingdom of the Atrebates, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verica">Verica</a>, recognised by Rome, was expelled. His exile gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> a pretext for invasion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 43 the invasion force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-plautius">Aulus Plautius</a> crossed in three divisions. The port of departure and the landing place are uncertain, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richborough">Richborough</a> in Kent is the usual choice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togodumnus">Togodumnus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a>, sons of the late king Cunobeline, led the resistance, and the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a> lasted two days. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> joined the army for the final march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, and eleven British kings are recorded as surrendering to him. Vespasian then took a force westward as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>, and by 47 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> probably held the land south-east of a line from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> to the Severn.</p><p>The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-ostorius-scapula">Publius Ostorius Scapula</a> fought the tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> from late 47, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a>, defeated, fled to the Brigantes, whose queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a> handed him over. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> attacked the druids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> in 60, but the rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> were destroyed, called him back to the south-east. It was put down by 61. Wales was finally subdued by about 77, and Cerialis and Frontinus advanced against the Brigantes. Gnaeus Julius Agricola, governor from 78, pushed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, defeated a Caledonian army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calgacus">Calgacus</a> at Mons Graupius and was recalled in 84.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a>'s successors abandoned much of what he had taken, and the fortress at Inchtuthil was dismantled before it was finished. By 87 the frontier was settled on the Stanegate road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> and Corbridge. Hadrian's Wall was begun from around 122, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a>, built by 142, was given up in 162. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> campaigned against the Caledonian Confederacy in 209, and Constantius led an army north in 306.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman conquest of Britain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
